ence). So differing positions are 0 and 2. So 2 positions, which is even. So swaps needed is 1. Because swapping positions 0 and 2 would fix both differences. So the answer is 1.\n\nSo the formula seems to be: if the number of differing positions is even, then the answer is (number of differing positions) // 2. Otherwise, it\'s impossible.\n\nSo the steps for the function would be:\n\n- Check if the lengths of the two strings are equal. If not, return "Not Possible".\n\n- Check if the counts of 0s and 1s are the same in both strings. If not, return "Not Possible".\n\n- Then, count the number of differing positions.\n\n- If the count is even, return count // 2. Else, return "Not Possible".\n\nWait, but in the case where the counts are the same, but the number of differing positions is even. So for example, if A is "1010" and B is "0101", which has 4 differing positions (even), so swaps needed is 2. Which is correct.\n\nSo the function would be:\n\ndef min_Swaps(A, B):\n\n    if len(A) != len(B):\n\n        return "Not Possible"\n\n    if A.count(\'0\') != B.count(\'0\') or A.count(\'1\') != B.count(\'1\'):\n\n        return "Not Possible"\n\n    differing = 0\n\n    for i in range(len(A)):\n\n        if A[i] != B[i]:\n\n            differing +=1\n\n    if differing % 2 != 0:\n\n        return "Not Possible"\n\n    return differing // 2\n\nWait, but what if the differing positions are not in pairs? For example, if there are two differing positions, but they are not in a pair. But according to the above logic, if the number is even, then it\'s possible. But how?\n\nWait, the code above would return differing // 2. But how?\n\nLet me think again. Suppose A is "1010" and B is "0101". The differing positions are 0,1,2,3. So differing is 4. So 4//2=2. Which is correct.\n\nBut what if the differing positions are 0 and 1, but the 0 in position 0 needs to be swapped with 1 in position 1. Then swapping them would fix both differences. So the code returns 1, which is correct.\n