n):\n    try:\n        if n < 0:\n            n = abs(n)\n        if n == 0:\n            return True\n        digits = len(str(n))\n        sum_power = sum(int(digit) ** digits for digit in str(n))\n        return sum_power == n\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        return None\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Summary\n\n- **Function Name**: `armstrong_number`\n- **Input**: Single integer `n`\n- **Output**: Boolean `True` or `False` (returns `None` if a `KeyboardInterrupt` occurs)\n- **Properties**:\n  - Handles negative numbers correctly.\n  - Special case for `0` (it is an Armstrong number).\n  - Uses `len(str(n))` to determine the number of digits.\n  - Returns `None` on `KeyboardInterrupt`.\n  - Passes all linter checks.\n\nThis solution is safe, efficient, and aligns with the company\'s coding standards.'
